vyrvannaya-stranitsa1-name = torn page
vyrvannaya-stranitsa1-desc = looks like someone's diary
vyrvannaya-stranitsa1-content = [bold]Day 11[/bold]

    {"At nightfall a rumble came from the graveyard - not wind, not beast, but a heavy stirring of earth, as though all the dead had turned at once in their coffins."}


    {"Enough! I will not hide behind doors with a psalter any longer. Am I a priest, or a mangy dog? I may be poor in battle, and my hand may know only censer and cross, but a cross is a weapon too."}

akkuramnye-zapusu-name = neat notes
akkuramnye-zapusu-desc = A sheet of fine parchment, pleasantly scented with dried herbs and field flowers. The handwriting is soft, rounded, and a little hurried.
akkuramnye-zapusu-content = [head=3][color=#228b22]Trade Register[/color][/head]


    {"[italic]Kept by Mary Leidemberg.[/italic]"}


    {"[bold]Third decade of the month.[/bold]"}


    {"Demand for healing salves and tinctures has risen again. On the one hand, as a merchant, I ought to rejoice at the profit. But my heart tightens every time I see weary travelers come to me bandaged. Oh, if they do not stop injuring themselves so thoughtlessly in skirmishes, I shall urgently need to order a double lot of healing herbs."}


    {"[bold]Deal with the Legion quartermaster:[/bold]"}

    {"The quartermaster tried again to bring down the price on a lot of elven cloaks, complaining of an empty treasury and hard times. I understand their burdens perfectly well, but I had to gently remind him: true elven silk does not rot in these awful marshes and keeps warmth beautifully even under freezing rain. It is far better than that coarse soldier's sackcloth, which soaks through to the thread."}

    {"Result: he agreed to the full price after all."}


    {"[bold]Goods written off:[/bold]"}

    {"Three vials of healing potion broken."}

    {"Cause: the loader boy was in such a hurry that he tripped over his own boot on flat ground. He looked at me so frightened that I felt terribly sorry for him. I wanted to forgive the whole thing, but trade requires discipline. I withheld part of the sum from his wages, leaving exactly enough for a hearty hot supper. In future, I shall ask someone nimbler to unload fragile vials, or perhaps do it myself."}


    {"[bold]Current instructions:[/bold]"}

    {"A shipment of rare spices and fabrics from the south is expected. It seems I shall have to spend coin on reliable protection for the shop after all. Yesterday, some grim people from the so-called \"Wolf Gang\" examined my display windows for far too long and far too closely. I dislike conflict with all my soul, but if any of them dare break into my chests, I have quite enough gold for mercenaries or the city watch to quickly beat the urge to steal out of them and throw them past the gates. I value the peace and safety of my shop too much to quarrel with brigands personally."}


    {"[italic]- page XII[/italic]"}

raport-starshego-doznavatelya-name = senior inquisitor's report
raport-starshego-doznavatelya-desc = Dusty parchment. The handwriting is even and elegant. The text drips with icy sarcasm and bureaucratic arrogance, just as one would expect from its author.
raport-starshego-doznavatelya-content = [head=3]REPORT ON AN INCIDENT IN THE TRADE QUARTER[/head]


    {"I have the dubious pleasure of reporting that the famed vigilance of our patrols is a fiction. This afternoon, a certain Gustav, whose intellect is inferior to the carcasses he chops, was deprived of his goods. The criminal did not merely clear out the stall, but entered the shop itself under the nose of its yawning owner. The stolen items were a smoked ham, a string of sausages, and a hefty brass meat hook. A souvenir, presumably."}


    {"According to the descriptions of dim-witted bystanders, we seek a dwarf of coal-black complexion. Red, inflamed eyes, and an insolent face radiant with absolute contempt for the law. The subject's hair is a black tow, ragged in every direction. His wardrobe would make even a cave savage wince: he is clothed in stinking torn rags and pieces of untanned hide."}


    {"Finding the trail of this relic bastard proved trivial. Informants in the port observed him selling the stolen goods to local fences. One buyer called him by name, but my hard-of-hearing informant remembered only fragments: it sounded like \"Rogvolod\", or perhaps \"Rygvold\", or possibly \"Ragvolot\"."}


    {"I recommend ceasing the polishing of boots on the parade ground and sending competent men to search the port flophouses. For the interrogation protocol in my cellars, the precise spelling of his name is immaterial."}

vyrvannaya-stranitsa2-name = torn page
vyrvannaya-stranitsa2-desc = Looks like someone's diary
vyrvannaya-stranitsa2-content = [bold]Day 1[/bold]

    {"A drunken peasant ran into the church at night, swearing he had seen a walking corpse in the graveyard. I called him possessed and threw him out."}


    {"In the morning I went to the village cemetery and could not believe my eyes. Oluf the Crooked's grave had been dug open - from within. The body is gone. A fox cannot do that."}


    {"I sprinkled the ground with holy water. Locked myself in my cell. If a dead man has risen, why would he flee? Lord, have mercy."}

vyrvannaya-stranitsa3-name = torn page
vyrvannaya-stranitsa3-desc = Looks like someone's diary
vyrvannaya-stranitsa3-content = [bold]Day 4[/bold]

    {"May the Lord have mercy."}


    {"Today the peasants returned from sowing and told a horror: living skeletons attacked them, fleshless, but with fierce bones. The men fought them off with hoes and flails, and so survived. Bruises and terror."}


    {"Our island is defiled. A grave was torn open, and now dead bones walk by themselves. Lord, why do you punish us? Tomorrow I shall order the candles left burning until morning."}


    {"The Devil is near."}

vyrvannaya-stranitsa4-name = torn page
vyrvannaya-stranitsa4-desc = Looks like someone's diary
vyrvannaya-stranitsa4-content = [bold]Day 7[/bold]

    {"Two layfolk vanished without a trace. No cry, no blood, not a shred of clothing - as if Satan himself had licked them away."}


    {"The village is weeping and trembling. I, a poor shepherd, do not know what to tell my flock. The Legate sits with folded hands, as though serving penance."}


    {"That grave is still empty. They say the skeletons no longer wander in pairs alone."}


    {"I lit the lamp before the Savior's face. I have nothing else with which to fight this darkness."}


    {"May God be with us. For if He has withdrawn, all is ended."}

stranitsa-iz-schetnoy-knigi-name = ledger page
stranitsa-iz-schetnoy-knigi-desc = A dense sheet of expensive paper, neatly cut from a ledger. Long columns of numbers line the margins, while the main text is written in a tiny, incredibly economical hand without a single unnecessary indent.
stranitsa-iz-schetnoy-knigi-content = [head=3][color=#b8860b]Notes on Business[/color][/head]


    {"[italic]The day brought a handsome profit, may I live so well! In the morning this schlimazel from the Legion dragged himself in wearing polished armor, asking for a loan to pay his cutthroats. Cried about hard times. Naturally I made a sorrowful face - we are respectable merchants, after all - but I tripled the rush interest. Stripped the dear boy to his last thread, pure gesheft! Took a promissory note too, secured against their port warehouses."}


    {"In the slums, every pauper whispers that we nocturnes are wild beasts, thinking only of tearing throats in alleyways. Oy, a zokhn vey, what primitive barbarism and damage to property! One must eat, of course. Without fresh blood we will surely stretch our legs. But why spoil the market value of a valuable debtor? Where is the tsimmes in that?"}


    {"Why send a milch cow to slaughter when one can carefully take a pint of blood from it toward unpaid penalties? The client breathes, I am fed, and profit rings in the chest. Today I squeezed both coin and a cup of fine Type O blood from the tavernkeeper. A pleasure to watch him pale. A clever blood-drinker works not with fangs, but with compound interest and a payment schedule!"}


    {"[bold]- Borislav Boyko[/bold][/italic]"}

okrovavlennyy-pergament2-name = bloodstained parchment
okrovavlennyy-pergament2-desc = The parchment is crumpled, the hand trembling and barely legible.
okrovavlennyy-pergament2-content = [center][head=2][color=#4a0000][bold]To my loved ones in Hafen[/bold][/color][/head][/center]


    {"If you are reading this, then I am gone. The hatch into the tower is nearly breached. I hear their scraping, that endless, maddening hiss. I have one crossbow bolt left. I will not give them the pleasure of taking my soul alive."}


    {"Forgive me. I will not return. In the next life I promise never to take up steel. I pray to the gods that at least there I do not end up in this living hell..."}


    {"[color=#8b0000]█████▓▓▒▒░[/color]"}

    {"[color=#8b0000]█████████▓▓▒░░[/color]"}

okrovavlennyy-pergament3-name = bloodstained parchment
okrovavlennyy-pergament3-desc = The page is covered in dark dried stains that hide part of the text.
okrovavlennyy-pergament3-content = [head=2][color=#8b0000]Watchman's Log[/color][/head]


    {"Scouts report strange movement in the fog from the ocean side. I hear [color=#8b0000]▓▒░██[/color] the clatter of bone rattles and hissing. Damned tribe..."}


    {"They are climbing straight out of [color=#8b0000]██▓▒░[/color]... we did not have time to load the cannons! Skeletons. Too many of them."}


    {"Strong walls will not save us when [color=#8b0000]█▓▒░[/color] crossbow bolts pass right through. They are already at [color=#8b0000]███▓▒░[/color]..."}


    {"[color=#8b0000]█████▓▒░░[/color]"}

ofitsialnyy-dokument-name = official document
ofitsialnyy-dokument-desc = A dense, stiff sheet of state paper. A heavy crimson wax seal of the Legion darkens the bottom. The text is written in a large, even clerk's hand, so a herald can read it easily from the scaffold. No emotion, only the cold and merciless letter of the law.
ofitsialnyy-dokument-content = [head=2]ACT OF THE MILITARY TRIBUNAL[/head]

    {"[italic]For public proclamation upon the scaffold[/italic]"}


    {"By this act, proclaimed before citizens and soldiers, the immutable sentence against former Decurion Vsevolod Temnov is affirmed."}


    {"This man, having forgotten duty and martial honor, violated the law. The Court of the Legion found him guilty of grievous crimes:"}


    {"Firstly, of vile bribery and extortion of coin beyond the established measure from honest merchants."}

    {"Secondly, of intimidation and direct threats against common folk, and of abetting night thieves."}

    {"Thirdly, of trampling his oath and betraying the trust of peaceful citizens, whom the Legion swore to defend."}


    {"For these crimes, the tribunal decrees: Vsevolod Temnov shall be stripped of rank, pay, and all regalia. He shall be put to death by beheading, so that the stain upon the Legion's honor may be washed away and all who would break the law may be warned."}


    {"[bold]This act is sealed and approved by the gracious Lord Legate. The sentence is to be carried out without delay![/bold]"}

vlazhnyy-pergament1-name = damp parchment
vlazhnyy-pergament1-desc = The parchment is soaked through with seawater. Salt has almost completely eaten away the cheap ink.
vlazhnyy-pergament1-content = T... c...mmand in the port of H...fen.

    {"S...a f...rt attacked. Creat...res of the Tr...be rose straight fr...m the oce...n floor. We are c...t off from shore."}


    {"[color=#4682b4]~~~~~~~~~ [washed out by water] ~~~~~~~~~[/color]"}


    {"...cannons are us...less. Grapesh...t does not take sk...letons. And their poiso...ed arrows cut our men down one by one."}


    {"I b...g you, send war gall...ys to h...lp! We are trapped on the upper ti...r. We will not h...ld long."}


    {"[color=#4682b4]~~~~~~~~~ [text washed away by a wave] ~~~~~~~~~[/color]"}

    {"...ory to the Leg...on!"}

vlazhnyy-pergament2-name = damp parchment
vlazhnyy-pergament2-desc = The parchment is soaked with moisture; drops of seawater have blurred the words.
vlazhnyy-pergament2-content = Half the garrison has f...llen. The poison from their arrows burns from with...n, and the healer says there are no healing p...tions left.


    {"They do not even try to br...ak the entrance doors - the amphibians climb straight up the smooth sheer w...lls and slip through the embras...res, while the sk...letons break the gr...tings below. The air reeks of s...a rot. We barric...ded the hatch upst..."}


    {"[color=#4682b4]~~~~~~~~~ [the ink ran] ~~~~~~~~~[/color]"}

    {"...with heavy cr...tes of ammunition."}

vlazhnyy-pergament3-name = damp parchment
vlazhnyy-pergament3-desc = The ink is badly blurred by seawater; half the text has vanished completely.
vlazhnyy-pergament3-content = They str...ck at d...wn. B...ny bastards are g...ing stra...ght at o...r cannons!

    {"[color=#4682b4]~~~~~~~~~ [blurred] ~~~~~~~~~[/color]"}

    {"...grapeshot p...sses betw...en the r...bs without h...rming them. Only d...rect hits from cannonballs t...ke them."}


    {"And behind the skeletons c...awl Lami...ns. Those tailed cre...ures are too f¤st. The lower tier is l...st. We are retre...ting upward."}

    {"[color=#4682b4]~~~~~~~~~ [text washed away by water] ~~~~~~~~~[/color]"}

obryvok-seroy-listovki-name = scrap of grey leaflet
obryvok-seroy-listovki-desc = A piece of cheap paper stuck up with foul-smelling glue. The letters are thick and crooked, as if written with a stub of charcoal, and the text itself reeks of tavern swagger and port alleyways.
obryvok-seroy-listovki-content = [head=3][color=#800000]HEY, LOWLIFES![/color][/head]


    {"[italic]Quit flapping your gums and breaking your back for Legion marks over a rotten crust! While those law-dogs stuff their bellies behind walls, you dying here in the port filth? Stop whining into your fist."}


    {"The Wolf Gang needs lucky, bold folk who do not fear the knife and take what is not offered. Our way is simple: strength in your hands, loot in the common chest. Want every roach in the slums to respect you, and proper coin to ring in your pocket? Want to eat your fill and roar in taverns?"}


    {"Come to the far piers, where the legionaries piss themselves before searching. Ask for Nikolai. If you are no victim and can put a blade under the right ribs, we will take you into the pack. With us, you will have coin and freedom."}


    {"Do not be stupid. Either you tear with the wolves, or you get sheared like a sheep."}


    {"[bold]- Nikolai Kostroving[/bold][/italic]"}

akkuratnoe-poslanie-name = neat message
akkuratnoe-poslanie-desc = A sheet of excellent vellum, covered in elegant, cramped handwriting without a single blot. A faint but persistent scent of formalin, dried herbs, and old bone rises from the letter.
akkuratnoe-poslanie-content = [head=3][color=#5a005a]To the Esteemed Archmage[/color][/head]

    {"[italic]My dear Magister, I am compelled to state that your current [italic]modus operandi[/italic] causes me the deepest academic and practical bewilderment. While you, surrendering to open hedonism and abundant libations in praise of Nexar, exhaust the Collegium's financial reserves, our magical barrier is [italic]de facto[/italic] in a state of progressive destruction."}


    {"Might I inquire where, precisely, the funds assigned for payment of hired contingents are settling? Owing to your permanent indifference and shortage of secutors, the prerogative of annihilating cursed growths has fallen wholly and entirely upon my shoulders. I must regularly leave the laboratory in order to personally dissect these malignant anomalies. I note, purely empirically, that permanent contact with Morbius's corruption has a rather specific effect upon my organism. The process of necrosis has already exposed the phalanges and forearm bones, though I confess there is a certain elegant, ascetic aesthetic in this reduction of flesh."}


    {"[italic]In summa[/italic], dear colleague: the barrier still functions, and your residence has avoided total infection exclusively thanks to my personal initiative, courage, and, if you permit, altruism. I strongly recommend that you minimize ethanol intoxication and return to administration. Otherwise I shall cease my philanthropy, and the process of fatal decomposition of the island will become irreversible."}


    {"[bold]- Rosello the Ominous[/bold][/italic]"}

depesha-legiona-name = Legion dispatch
depesha-legiona-desc = A dense sheet of first-rate paper, fastened with the impression of a heavy wax seal bearing the Legion's coat of arms. The text is composed in full military-bureaucratic form, concise and without unnecessary sentiment.
depesha-legiona-content = [head=3][color=#b22222]Official Dispatch of Legion Command[/color][/head]


    {"[italic]To: The Leadership of the Imperial Collegium of Mages."}

    {"From: Command of the Legion Garrison."}


    {"In the name of Emperor Therion, I demand an immediate consolidated report on the present state of the magical barrier. Reconnaissance patrols entrusted to me have recorded increased activity of dark forces and the emergence of corruption sites on the near approaches to our perimeter."}


    {"I remind you that the Legion has been assigned a strategic task of state importance: ensuring the region's security and uninterrupted mineral extraction. Any fluctuation of the protective field threatens the execution of the Emperor's order and the lives of personnel."}


    {"In light of this, I insist upon a joint inspection of all barrier nodes, as well as reinforcement of magical posts in potentially dangerous directions. Coordination between patrols and your specialists must be established as soon as possible."}


    {"The matter of maintaining absolute isolation of cursed zones is under my personal control. I expect an authorized representative of the Collegium with a report at tomorrow's military council."}


    {"[bold]- Legate of the Legion[/bold][/italic]"}

potemnevshiy-ot-vremeni-svitok-name = scroll darkened by time
potemnevshiy-ot-vremeni-svitok-desc = A long, dense scroll of fine parchment wound around carved bone rollers. It smells not of rot or ale, but only clean beeswax and old ink. The lines are written by the firm hand of an unknown chronicler who chose to set the island's tragedy into verse.
potemnevshiy-ot-vremeni-svitok-content = [head=3][color=#b8860b]Song of the Gods' Judgment[/color][/head]


    {"[italic]Once here a gentle light was cast,"}

    {"And Soma guarded quiet shore."}

    {"But purity fled into the past,"}

    {"And mist grew thick above the moor."}


    {"Imperial ships came through the foam,"}

    {"With picks they broke the mountain's womb."}

    {"Ore was the lure that led them home,"}

    {"Blind metal drew them to their doom."}


    {"Forgetting awe before the gods,"}

    {"The legionaries formed their line."}

    {"They fenced themselves with trenches, rods,"}

    {"But trenches do not bar decline."}


    {"The drained and weary barrier cracks,"}

    {"Mold eats the archives, tome by tome,"}

    {"And mages, following shameful tracks,"}

    {"Seek sleep in Nexar's wine at home."}


    {"But gods began their judgment then,"}

    {"Rousing wrath from depths below."}

    {"The watchfires roar on shores of men,"}

    {"And Valtor, lord of blood, shall go"}


    {"To lead the horde to dreadful war,"}

    {"Beneath the drums' infernal roll,"}

    {"Sweeping the frail ranks from the shore,"}

    {"And charms of weak talismans whole."}


    {"Yet worse than all misfortune's breath"}

    {"Came not from wild borderlands:"}

    {"A love that burned itself to death"}

    {"Opened the doors to shadowed hands."}


    {"The maddened count, to save his flesh,"}

    {"Let curse pass into his own hall."}

    {"And Morbius, with a tightening mesh,"}

    {"Snuffed life's last remnants in that fall."}


    {"The Dark now strides from under stone,"}

    {"Ripping shrouds from bones beneath."}

    {"Before eternity's cold throne"}

    {"Stand equal mage and man of wreath."}


    {"The broken barrier shall fall,"}

    {"Pride vanish, steel be overthrown."}

    {"And through the caverns, over all,"}

    {"Black, soundless sorrow takes the throne."}


    {"For this isle is no mine of ore,"}

    {"But cup of wrath and scales of fate."}

    {"And every soul who crossed its shore"}

    {"Shall know the gods' dread judgment late.[/italic]"}

pomyatyy-dorozhnyy-pergament-name = crumpled travel parchment
pomyatyy-dorozhnyy-pergament-desc = A dense sheet of parchment rolled into a tube and tied with a simple leather cord. The paper bears traces of long wandering: grey stains of campfire soot, rain marks, and a pair of wolf hairs stuck to it.
pomyatyy-dorozhnyy-pergament-content = [head=3][color=#2e8b57]Ballad of the Wanderer and the Wolf[/color][/head]


    {"[italic]Through woods where creeping shadows lie,"}

    {"And mist conceals the fading track,"}

    {"There walks, beneath the low grey sky,"}

    {"One sworn to roads and never back."}


    {"Lukelian is the name he bears,"}

    {"A plain man worn by life and rain."}

    {"The wind has torn the coat he wears,"}

    {"Yet darkened cold brings him no pain."}


    {"Beside him, shadow under trees,"}

    {"His faithful grey brother treads."}

    {"Among these cursed and bitter days,"}

    {"He is more than comrade where he leads."}


    {"That wolf is called Sharik by name,"}

    {"No savage beast, but loyal ward."}

    {"He knows the dark paths all the same,"}

    {"And flares to battle swift and hard."}


    {"Together they passed cordons wide,"}

    {"Where sentries blindly drink and snore,"}

    {"Watched bastions rot from tide to tide,"}

    {"And wild men drive their slaves before."}


    {"When in the night the war-host cries,"}

    {"And Valtor calls for gifts of gore,"}

    {"No fear nor gloom can close their eyes,"}

    {"They skirt the fires and pass once more."}


    {"When bones rise up from under earth,"}

    {"By dead men's sorcery made to stir,"}

    {"Sharik bares fangs for all their worth,"}

    {"And Lukelian strikes through smoke and burr."}


    {"They need no power, name, or praise,"}

    {"No gold torn from the mountain's vein."}

    {"For them, reward enough always"}

    {"Is one more day upon the lane."}


    {"Let the isle die of old disgrace,"}

    {"Let gods pronounce their judgment due."}

    {"Through ash and rot and wind's embrace"}

    {"Man and wolf keep passing through.[/italic]"}

nadushennyy-pergament2-name = perfumed parchment
nadushennyy-pergament2-desc = The handwriting is elegant, but the letters are drawn with such irritated pressure that the quill nearly pierced the paper in places. A sharp floral perfume rises from the sheet - clearly an attempt to drown out the tavern's ingrained stench.
nadushennyy-pergament2-content = [head=3]Complaint Regarding Quality of Service[/head]


    {"My good sir. I consider it my duty to enlighten your meager mind regarding yesterday's incident. And if you or any of your regulars dare once more to blurt that Sietti Veylans \"got drunk from two glasses and fell asleep in the salad\", I shall personally nail your tongue to the bar with a crossbow bolt."}


    {"Permit me to explain [italic]post factum[/italic]: my sudden... loss of coordination during the performance of the ballad was by no means common drunkenness. That is [italic]a priori[/italic] impossible. My refined nocturne metabolism was merely attempting to reject that aggressive poison, steeped in rotten plums and swamp sludge, which you had the audacity to serve me under the guise of a noble drink. They say [italic]in vino veritas[/italic]; well, the truth in your wine is fit for killing rats in cellars."}


    {"It is a miracle I managed to keep hold of my instrument at all. My lute is worth more than your entire lineage, and you force me to play in a hall where it stinks [italic]ad nauseam[/italic] of unwashed boots and sour mash."}


    {"As recompense for poisoning me and for the insult to my person, before sunset you will send to my room a jug of proper, undiluted southern wine and a plate of hot meat. From this day onward I pay not a copper coin for food in your establishment."}


    {"Otherwise my [italic]modus operandi[/italic] shall change sharply, and I shall submit an official paper to the Legate. I assure you, when Legion command learns that you are making the garrison and noble guests drunk on outright poison, your tavern will be closed [italic]de jure[/italic] and [italic]de facto[/italic]. You yourself will be sent to knead mud in the swamps. And believe me, watching your ruin will bring me far greater aesthetic pleasure than playing for swine in your hall."}


    {"[italic]- Sietti Veylans[/italic]"}

grubyy-pergament-name = rough parchment
grubyy-pergament-desc = A sheet of cheap, dense paper, slightly worn at the edges. The handwriting is large, clumsy, and uneven, with fat blots of cheap ink visible in places. The letter itself smells distinctly of barn dust.
grubyy-pergament-content = [head=3]To His Grace the Legate[/head]

    {"[italic]From Ipat, honest merchant[/italic]"}


    {"Do not be angered, your grace, that I tear you from important matters. But there is nowhere else to seek the truth. Meaning, your legionaries are decent men, known to all. Our protection and our pillar. That is why it sickens me to write this complaint against your man, the decurion, you know, Vsevolod Temnov."}


    {"This decurion, see, has taken to coming round my barns every seventh day. And always he wants coin above what is owed to the treasury. He calls it a fee for protection. And if I do not give it, he says, the patrolmen will start giving my street a wide berth. And goods, he says, may by chance be set aflame, or night thieves may ruin them."}


    {"I have always traded honestly, but with such greed of his I shall go into the world with a beggar's sack and beg. Have mercy, your grace, rein in this extortioner, and I will pray for your health all my days."}


    {"[bold]With the greatest respect.[/bold]"}

ochen-staroe-pismo-name = very old letter
ochen-staroe-pismo-content = Mother,


    {"Forgive me that this letter will come to your table in secret, like a shadow. When you read it, I will already be on the road - to the old estate of grandfather and grandmother on your side, where black moss creeps up to the very doors and the air smells of peat and ancient vows. I kept silent for long, listening at councils to speeches of heresy, but I can no longer. I accept the laws of the Empire banning the school of Dark Art neither in heart nor in mind. You whispered to me by the hearth that darkness is not wickedness, but reins holding the abyss away from the unworthy. I remembered. The council forgot."}


    {"Father is guilty of nothing. He, archivist of the Collegium, only answered my pleas and unlocked the book vaults when I swore to him that I sought protection for the world, not forbidden power. If accusation falls, let it fall upon me, not upon his grey hairs."}


    {"I have resolved to master this art myself, without your help and without the Collegium's scepter. The old castle in the swamps will become my home and laboratory. If I do not comprehend dark matter, if I do not learn to speak with spirits in the language by which they weave oblivion, the island is finished. Our whole world is finished. Morbius does not wait while archmages argue over dogma."}


    {"Goldur the Wise will be beside me. He loves me, mother, and his elemental power will guard my back when I step beyond a threshold from which no one returns unchanged. He will not let me perish in the bogs - neither the marshy nor the astral ones."}


    {"Do not curse my choice. I leave so that one day, when eternity has heard my arguments, I may return to the castle gates and show you that the Dark Art can be a vow, not a curse. Until that day, listen to the wind: it will carry the thread of my steps through the astral."}


    {"Your daughter forever,"}

    {"Keri"}


    {"P.S. And be careful... You are far too busy, but I am certain you simply do not notice that your slippery half-mage adviser Dimitrus, with his eternal sly squint, is working under you! At any moment he will frame and betray you. Please, be careful, and do not let him blacken your name!"}

poterennaya-istoriya-name = lost story
poterennaya-istoriya-desc = this sheet was written long ago by some novice writer, but then vanished without a trace...
poterennaya-istoriya-content = >+
    {"The city of Legion was famous for its bureaucracy and its right angles. So when the old wooden pier rotted away, the Legion Council, without overthinking, assigned construction of a new port to a local carpenter whom everyone simply called Plane. The task was simple: chop, nail together, put up a numbered sign."}


    {"Trouble came from where no one expected it - the sign. Plane was not merely a carpenter; he was a mystic. He believed a number determined the fate of wood. For weeks he lay on boards, staring at the grey sky and moving his lips."}


    {"\"Number Six,\" he muttered, tapping his forehead with a hand plane. \"Six is stability. It is two by three. It is a nail that cannot be pulled out. The port will stand a thousand years! But...\" he rolled onto his side, and the boards creaked pitifully. \"Six is too smooth. Boring. The Legion does not like boredom.\""}


    {"\"Number Seven!\" he would leap up, waving a chisel. \"Seven is magic, seven hills of Rome, seven days of creation. But seven is odd. And an odd port in an even city is like a crookedly driven peg. All the cargo will lean left! Merchants will fall into the water!\""}


    {"Construction stalled. Workers slept on the piles, gulls made nests in the unfinished cash desks, and Plane sat on the shore throwing chips into the water, muttering: \"Six sinks faster, seven floats longer, but where to?\""}


    {"And then revelation struck him. A brilliant solution meant to please both legionaries and gods."}


    {"He burst into the prefect's office, wet with sweat and inspiration."}

    {"\"I have decided! Not six and not seven! Let it be Sixty-Seven! 67! It is grandeur! It is stable magic! It is two prime numbers walking hand in hand! The port will be the greatest in history!\""}


    {"The prefect, who was eating dried fish at the time, chewed for a long while and finally pronounced:"}

    {"\"Fine. Make it 67. Just be quick.\""}


    {"Plane ran out, full of enthusiasm. He personally carved an enormous board with the numbers \"67\" and coated it with three layers of resin. He nailed it to the port's central arch with such passion that the hammer sparked."}


    {"And at the very moment the last nail entered the wood, the inevitable happened."}


    {"The sky over Legion darkened. Winds from the sea joined into one mighty gust, the kind carpenters call a \"widow's sigh\". The piles, which had waited three months for their fate, bent all at once. The boards on which Plane had lain while pondering destiny burst into splinters with a crack. And the sign with the number \"67\" tore free, performed a pirouette in the air, struck Plane on the back of the head, and fell into the sea with a splash."}


    {"The port collapsed at the exact moment it was completed. It folded like a house of cards, not from poor timber, but from the very indecision that had gathered in every knot."}


    {"When the legionaries came running, they saw amid the wreckage only Plane's finger sticking out of the sand, twitching nervously between \"six\" and \"seven\". The carpenter himself sat on a broken mast, mumbling as he stared at the fallen sign already carried away by the waves:"}


    {"\"Five?.. Eight?.. Gods, it turned over! It is floating upside down! Now it is not clear at all what it is!\""}


    {"The port was eventually finished a year later by other carpenters. They simply nailed together a shed and put up a sign marked \"A\". Plane was appointed keeper of the lighthouse - the oldest lighthouse, which, incidentally, had long had no number. He still sits there every night, whispering to the wind:"}


    {"\"If I had chosen 6, the fall would have been softer. If I had chosen 7, I would have been carried out to sea, but not so shamefully. But 67... 67 broke my heart...\""}


    {"What number did that ill-fated object receive in the end? In the city chronicles, it is listed as \"Port No. (illegible)\", because the ink on the paper blurred from damp that very day. And to this day, local fishermen cross themselves when they pass by and say: \"Do not divine by numbers, carpenter. The gods like it when a sign is simply nailed up.\""}

